ing Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS"],"anchor":"getting-started-with-tail-os","preview":"Welcome to TAIL OS! This guide helps you run TailOS, set up a development environment, and build your own applications. Working on TAIL OS itself (kernel, drivers, build system) rather than just running it? See [Develope"},{"id":"getting-started#table-of-contents:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Table of Contents"],"anchor":"table-of-contents","preview":"1. [Prerequisites](prerequisites) 2. [Run TailOS](run-tailos) 3. [Developer setup](developer-setup) 4. [Developing Applications](developing-applications) 5. [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting)"},{"id":"getting-started#system-requirements:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Prerequisites","System Requirements"],"anchor":"system-requirements","preview":"- Operating System: Ubuntu 22.04 or 24.04 (22.04 is the validated baseline for building from source — the toolchain build pins gcc-11) - Architecture: x86_64 host system - Memory: 4 GB to run the prebuilt image; 8 GB rec"},{"id":"getting-started#run-tailos:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Run TailOS"],"anchor":"run-tailos","preview":"Boot TailOS in QEMU in about two minutes — no toolchain, no build, no clone. 1. Install QEMU (the only requirement): bash sudo apt install -y qemu-system-aarch64 qemu-utils 2. Launch it with the one-command launcher. On "},{"id":"getting-started#developer-setup:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Developer setup"],"anchor":"developer-setup","preview":"Want to change TailOS, or build apps against the current source? The developer bootstrap turns a fresh Ubuntu machine into a complete TailOS build environment with one script. You run it once; it takes ~2–3 hours, mostly"},{"id":"getting-started#developing-applications:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Developing Applications"],"anchor":"developing-applications","preview":"A TailOS application is an ordinary Rust std program — a normal fn main() with the full standard library. Only the kernel is no_std. You compile it for the aarch64-unknown-tail target with the tail_release toolchain, cop"},{"id":"getting-started#1-create-the-project:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Developing Applications","1. Create the project"],"anchor":"1-create-the-project","preview":"bash cargo new hello-tail cd hello-tail The default src/main.rs already works unchanged: rust fn main() { println!(\"Hello, TailOS!\"); }"},{"id":"getting-started#2-select-the-tailos-toolchain:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Developing Applications","2. Select the TailOS toolchain"],"anchor":"2-select-the-tailos-toolchain","preview":"Pin the custom toolchain for this project directory (sub-directories inherit it): bash rustup override set tail_release Equivalently, pass +tail_release to each cargo invocation."},{"id":"getting-started#3-build-for-the-tailos-target:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Developing Applications","3. Build for the TailOS target"],"anchor":"3-build-for-the-tailos-target","preview":"bash cargo build --target aarch64-unknown-tail --release No extra linker flags are needed: the aarch64-unknown-tail target spec bakes the whole user-space link recipe — the link script, the self-contained tail_crt0.o sta"},{"id":"getting-started#4-copy-the-binary-onto-the-tailos-disk:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Developing Applications","4. Copy the binary onto the TailOS disk"],"anchor":"4-copy-the-binary-onto-the-tailos-disk","preview":"make run-release boots the tail_disk.img in the repository root and mounts its data partition as /. Add your binary to that image with the same tool the build uses — no sudo, no manual mounting. Create a one-line deploy "},{"id":"getting-started#5-run-it:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Developing Applications","5. Run it"],"anchor":"5-run-it","preview":"From the tailos repo: bash make run-release Wait for the /$ prompt, then: /$ ls /usr/bin /$ /usr/bin/hello-tail Hello, TailOS! Exit QEMU with Ctrl-A, then X. Re-running make build-release re-deploys the OS's own utilitie"},{"id":"getting-started#6-using-tailos-services-from-your-app:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Developing Applications","6. Using TailOS services from your app"],"anchor":"6-using-tailos-services-from-your-app","preview":"For anything beyond the standard library — IPC topics, talking to OS servers, device memory — TailOS std re-exports the platform crate tail_core. Enable it with the tail_core feature; no extra Cargo dependency is require"},{"id":"getting-started#publish-subscribe-topic-ipc:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Developing Applications","6. Using TailOS services from your app","Publish / subscribe (topic IPC)"],"anchor":"publish-subscribe-topic-ipc","preview":"Topics are asynchronous pub/sub. Publishing never blocks, and read() is non-blocking — it returns None when no message is queued, so a subscriber polls on its own schedule: rust ![feature(tail_core)] use std::tail_core::"},{"id":"getting-started#calling-an-os-service-request-reply:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Developing Applications","6. Using TailOS services from your app","Calling an OS service (request / reply)"],"anchor":"calling-an-os-service-request-reply","preview":"Servers (process_manager, the FAT filesystem, TTY, …) are reached by name through the service client. This is the exact call pidls makes to query process_manager for the running-process list: rust ![feature(tail_core)] u"},{"id":"getting-started#memory:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Developing Applications","6. Using TailOS services from your app","Memory"],"anchor":"memory","preview":"Use the standard heap — Vec, Box, String, etc. — for ordinary allocation; it behaves exactly as on any std platform. The lower-level std::tail_core::memory::mmap::mmap_physical_memory(base, size, prot) is for mapping phy"},{"id":"getting-started#7-debugging:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Developing Applications","7. Debugging"],"anchor":"7-debugging","preview":"Build a debug binary and deploy it the same way (step 4), then start QEMU with the GDB stub from the tailos repo: bash cargo build --target aarch64-unknown-tail debug profile (no --release) ...deploy with deploy_disk.py "},{"id":"getting-started#build-failures:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Troubleshooting","Common Issues","Build Failures"],"anchor":"build-failures","preview":"bash Clean and rebuild make clean make build-debug"},{"id":"getting-started#qemu-issues:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Troubleshooting","Common Issues","QEMU Issues"],"anchor":"qemu-issues","preview":"bash Check QEMU installation qemu-system-aarch64 --version Try different QEMU options qemu-system-aarch64 -M raspi3b -kernel ${TAIL_PREFIX}/os_image/tail.rfs -serial stdio"},{"id":"getting-started#rust-toolchain-issues:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Troubleshooting","Common Issues","Rust Toolchain Issues"],"anchor":"rust-toolchain-issues","preview":"bash Update Rust rustup update Reinstall target rustup target remove aarch64-unknown-tail rustup target add aarch64-unknown-tail"},{"id":"getting-started#cross-compiler-issues:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Troubleshooting","Common Issues","Cross-Compiler Issues"],"anchor":"cross-compiler-issues","preview":"bash Rebuild toolchain cd toolchain/aarch64-elf-gcc ./build_target_toolchain.sh"},{"id":"getting-started#getting-help:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Troubleshooting","Getting Help"],"anchor":"getting-help","preview":"- Documentation: Check doc/ directory for detailed documentation - Issues: Report bugs and ask questions on the project repository - Community: Join discussions in project forums"},{"id":"getting-started#development-tips:0","docSlug":"getting-started","docTitle":"Getting Started","headingPath":["Getting Started with TAIL OS","Troubleshooting","Development Tips"],"anchor":"development-tips","preview":"1. Start Simple: Begin with basic applications before complex ones 2. Use Debug Builds: Debug builds include more error checking 3. Test Frequently: Test your applications regularly during development 4. Read Documentati"},{"id":"qemu#running-tailos-in-qemu:0","docSlug":"qemu","docTitle":"Running TAIL OS in QEMU","headingPath":["Running TailOS in QEMU"],"anchor":"running-tailos-in-qemu","preview":"Try TailOS on your Linux machine in under two minutes — no hardware, no toolchain. For installing TailOS on real Raspberry Pi 3 hardware, see [install_rpi3.md](install_rpi3.md)."},{"id":"qemu#prerequisites:0","docSlug":"qemu","docTitle":"Running TAIL OS in QEMU","headingPath":["Running TailOS in QEMU","Prerequisites"],"anchor":"prerequisites","preview":"| Item | Install command | |------|-----------------| | Linux host (Ubuntu 22.04 / 24.04 tested) | — | | qemu-system-aarch64 | sudo apt-get install -y qemu-system-aarch64 qemu-utils | | curl or wget | usually preinstalle"},{"id":"qemu#run-tailos-one-command:0","docSlug":"qemu","docTitle":"Running TAIL OS in QEMU","headingPath":["Running TailOS in QEMU","Run TailOS (one command)"],"anchor":"run-tailos-one-command","preview":"bash curl -sSL (private repository) | bash The launcher will: 1. Check that QEMU is installed. 2. Download tail.rfs (kernel) and tail_disk.img (data disk) into ~/.cache/tailos/ on first run. Later runs reuse the cache. 3"},{"id":"qemu#try-the-shell:0","docSlug":"qemu","docTitle":"Running TAIL OS in QEMU","headingPath":["Running TailOS in QEMU","Try the Shell"],"anchor":"try-the-shell","preview":"/$ help /$ pwd /$ ls /usr/bin /$ /usr/bin/pidls /$ cd /usr/bin /usr/bin$ exit The prompt shows the current directory (it changes after cd). The shell also supports arrow-key history, per-process cwd, and background jobs "},{"id":"qemu#manual-launch-alternative:0","docSlug":"qemu","docTitle":"Running TAIL OS in QEMU","headingPath":["Running TailOS in QEMU","Manual Launch (alternative)"],"anchor":"manual-launch-alternative","preview":"If you prefer not to run a remote script, do it by hand: bash 1. Install QEMU sudo apt-get install -y qemu-system-aarch64 qemu-utils 2. Download the images wget https://tail-os.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/tail.rfs wge"},{"id":"qemu#whats-inside-the-images:0","docSlug":"qemu","docTitle":"Running TAIL OS in QEMU","headingPath":["Running TailOS in QEMU","What's Inside the Images"],"anchor":"whats-inside-the-images","preview":"tail.rfs is a custom ELF container that bundles the entire OS: tail.rfs ├── startup_rpi3 Early boot (EL3 - EL1, MMU, jump to kernel) ├── kernel Microkernel (process, thread, IPC, scheduler) ├── uart_pl011 UART + TTY serv"},{"id":"qemu#boot-sequence:0","docSlug":"qemu","docTitle":"Running TAIL OS in QEMU","headingPath":["Running TailOS in QEMU","Boot Sequence"],"anchor":"boot-sequence","preview":"qemu-system-aarch64 -M raspi3b | v QEMU loads tail.rfs at 0x80000 (raspi3b entry point) | v TailOS startup (_start.S) |-- Switch EL3 - EL1 |-- Initialize MMU, caches |-- Jump to kernel v TailOS kernel |-- Initialize proc"},{"id":"qemu#troubleshooting:0","docSlug":"qemu","docTitle":"Running TAIL OS in QEMU","headingPath":["Running TailOS in QEMU","Troubleshooting"],"anchor":"troubleshooting","preview":"qemu-system-aarch64: command not found: Install QEMU with sudo apt-get install -y qemu-system-aarch64 qemu-utils. No output at all in the QEMU window: The launcher passes -serial mon:stdio for you. If you're running QEMU"},{"id":"qemu#see-also:0","docSlug":"qemu","docTitle":"Running TAIL OS in QEMU","headingPath":["Running TailOS in QEMU","See Also"],"anchor":"see-also","preview":"- [install_rpi3.md](install_rpi3.md) — Installing on Raspberry Pi 3 hardware - [buildsystem.md](buildsystem.md) — Building TailOS from source (contributors)"},{"id":"raspberry-pi-3#hardware-required:0","docSlug":"raspberry-pi-3","docTitle":"Installing on Raspberry Pi 3","headingPath":["Installing TailOS on Raspberry Pi 3","Hardware Required"],"anchor":"hardware-required","preview":"| Item | Notes | |------|-------| | Raspberry Pi 3B or 3B+ | | | MicroSD card (8 GB+) | | | USB-to-TTL serial adapter (3.3V) | For serial console (FTDI, CP2102, etc.) | | 5V / 2.5A power supply | Official RPi PSU recomme"},{"id":"raspberry-pi-3#quick-start:0","docSlug":"raspberry-pi-3","docTitle":"Installing on Raspberry Pi 3","headingPath":["Installing TailOS on Raspberry Pi 3","Quick Start"],"anchor":"quick-start","preview":"bash 1. Build TailOS and create the SD card image make sd-image 2. Flash to SD card (replace /dev/sdX with your SD card device) sudo dd if=tailos_sd.img of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress sync 3. Wire serial console, inse"},{"id":"raspberry-pi-3#serial-console-wiring:0","docSlug":"raspberry-pi-3","docTitle":"Installing on Raspberry Pi 3","headingPath":["Installing TailOS on Raspberry Pi 3","Serial Console Wiring"],"anchor":"serial-console-wiring","preview":"Connect the USB-to-TTL serial adapter to the RPi3 GPIO header: USB-TTL Adapter RPi3 GPIO Header +-----------+ +----------------+ | | | | | TX o----+-----------| Pin 10 (RXD) | GPIO 15 | | | | | RX o<---+-----------o| Pin"},{"id":"raspberry-pi-3#sd-card-image-structure:0","docSlug":"raspberry-pi-3","docTitle":"Installing on Raspberry Pi 3","headingPath":["Installing TailOS on Raspberry Pi 3","SD Card Image Structure"],"anchor":"sd-card-image-structure","preview":"The make sd-image command produces tailos_sd.img with the following layout: +===========================================================================+ | tailos_sd.img (193 MiB) | +====================================="},{"id":"raspberry-pi-3#boot-sequence:0","docSlug":"raspberry-pi-3","docTitle":"Installing on Raspberry Pi 3","headingPath":["Installing TailOS on Raspberry Pi 3","Boot Sequence"],"anchor":"boot-sequence","preview":"Power On | v GPU ROM - loads bootcode.bin from boot partition | v bootcode.bin - loads start.elf | v start.elf - reads config.txt, loads kernel8.img at 0x80000 | v TailOS startup (_start.S) |-- Switch EL3 - EL1 |-- Initi"},{"id":"raspberry-pi-3#build-details:0","docSlug":"raspberry-pi-3","docTitle":"Installing on Raspberry Pi 3","headingPath":["Installing TailOS on Raspberry Pi 3","Build Details"],"anchor":"build-details","preview":"The make sd-image target: 1. Builds TailOS (make build-release) 2. Downloads RPi3 firmware from raspberrypi/firmware (cached locally) 3. Converts tail.rfs (ELF) to kernel8.img (raw binary) via objcopy -O binary 4. Create"},{"id":"raspberry-pi-3#offline-firmware:0","docSlug":"raspberry-pi-3","docTitle":"Installing on Raspberry Pi 3","headingPath":["Installing TailOS on Raspberry Pi 3","Build Details","Offline Firmware"],"anchor":"offline-firmware","preview":"To use pre-downloaded firmware instead of auto-downloading: bash python3 utility/host/deploy_disk/make_sd_image.py \\ ./tail.build \\ $TAIL_PREFIX/os_image/tail.rfs \\ $TAIL_PREFIX/target/bin \\ tailos_sd.img \\ --firmware-di"},{"id":"raspberry-pi-3#configtxt-reference:0","docSlug":"raspberry-pi-3","docTitle":"Installing on Raspberry Pi 3","headingPath":["Installing TailOS on Raspberry Pi 3","config.txt Reference"],"anchor":"configtxt-reference","preview":"The generated config.txt contains: | Setting | Value | Purpose | |---------|-------|---------| | arm_64bit | 1 | Boot in AArch64 mode | | kernel | kernel8.img | Kernel filename to load | | disable_commandline_tags | 1 | "},{"id":"raspberry-pi-3#troubleshooting:0","docSlug":"raspberry-pi-3","docTitle":"Installing on Raspberry Pi 3","headingPath":["Installing TailOS on Raspberry Pi 3","Troubleshooting"],"anchor":"troubleshooting","preview":"No serial output: - Verify TX/RX wiring (they must be crossed: adapter TX - RPi RX) - Ensure baud rate is 115200 - Check that the SD card has config.txt with dtoverlay=disable-bt and enable_uart=1 RPi power LED on but no"}]}